## 5. Application Layer Structure (Vertical Slices)

Instead of grouping by technical type (`Services/`, `DTOs/`, `Validators/`), each **feature** gets its own folder containing everything it needs. This keeps related code together and makes each use case easy to find, read, and test in isolation.

```
StudyHub.Application/
├── Common/
│   ├── Interfaces/          → IUserRepository, ICourseRepository, IAiService...
│   ├── Behaviors/           → ValidationBehavior.cs (MediatR pipeline step)
│   └── Exceptions/          → NotFoundException, ValidationException...
│
├── Courses/
│   ├── Commands/
│   │   ├── CreateCourse/
│   │   │   ├── CreateCourseCommand.cs
│   │   │   ├── CreateCourseCommandHandler.cs
│   │   │   └── CreateCourseCommandValidator.cs
│   │   └── ArchiveCourse/
│   │       ├── ArchiveCourseCommand.cs
│   │       └── ArchiveCourseCommandHandler.cs
│   └── Queries/
│       └── GetCoursesByUser/
│           ├── GetCoursesByUserQuery.cs
│           ├── GetCoursesByUserQueryHandler.cs
│           └── CourseDto.cs
│
├── Tasks/          → same pattern (Commands + Queries)
├── Notes/          → same pattern
└── Users/          → Register, Login, RefreshToken commands
```

**How a request flows end-to-end:**
1. API endpoint receives an HTTP request, builds a `Command`/`Query` object, calls `mediator.Send(...)`.
2. MediatR pipeline runs `ValidationBehavior` first — it finds the matching FluentValidation `Validator` for that Command/Query and runs it. If invalid → throws before the handler ever runs.
3. If valid, MediatR routes to the matching `Handler`, which talks to the repository interfaces and returns a result.
4. API endpoint maps the result to an HTTP response.

This means a controller/endpoint method stays 3–5 lines, always.

